.TH "WSLDOCTOR" "1" "DATEPLACEHOLDER" "VERSIONPLACEHOLDER" "WSL Utilities User Manual"
.SH NAME
.B wsldoctor
- Component of WSL Utilities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wsldoctor
.RB [ \-\-fix ]
.RB [ \-hv ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run self-checks on the WSL environment wslu depends on and print a PASS/WARN/FAIL report with fix suggestions. Checks: WSL interop availability, the reachable Windows System32 directory, Windows PowerShell, the Windows build number (1903 or newer is required for converting Linux paths with \fBwslpath \-w\fR), the Local intranet zone mapping for the \fI\\\\wsl.localhost\fR and \fIwsl$\fR share hosts, whether the wslview helper port file points at a live helper (a stale file can hang wslview under mirrored networking), the wslview --on file server and tunnel state (a stale pid file, a server not answering on its recorded port, dead tunnel sockets, orphaned port records), the target inbox where copied files land (entries older than \fBWSLVIEW_ON_INBOX_TTL\fR days are pruned by the next copy call), and the validity of wslu configuration values.
.PP
Without options the doctor only reports and prints the commands that fix each problem. With \fB\-\-fix\fR, safe and idempotent fixes are applied first and reported: the intranet zone entries are added, a stale wslview helper port file is removed, and stale wslview --on server and tunnel state is cleaned up. Inbox entries are only reported, never removed automatically. Re-run the doctor after restarting WSL to confirm a fix took effect.
.PP
Exit status: 0 when every check passes, 1 when at least one check reports WARN or FAIL. A running wslview file server is reported as WARN as a notice, not a fault.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --fix
Apply safe fixes after the checks.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print a simple help.
.TP
.B -v, --version
Print current version.
